\section{Introduction}
\noindent Digital platforms have made information widely accessible and have transformed how people communicate, learn, and participate in public life. However, the same mechanisms that allow useful information to spread quickly also allow false or misleading information to reach large audiences. On social media, misinformation can be produced, amplified, and disseminated at high speed, sometimes contributing to confusion, mistrust, or social unrest.

The widespread use of social media has made misinformation easier to generate and harder to contain~\cite{CHEN2023107643}. Automated accounts and coordinated bot activity can increase the visibility of misleading content and allow it to reach large audiences rapidly~\cite{info:doi/10.2196/26933}. In addition, misinformation often uses emotional or attention-grabbing language, which can increase user engagement and further amplify its spread~\cite{lewandowskyDebunkingHandbook20202020}. Repeated exposure to false claims can also make them appear more credible, making social media platforms particularly vulnerable to manipulation. As a result, misinformation has become an increasingly important challenge in recent years~\cite{ebsco_misinformation_international,bilodeau2024misinformation}.

Mitigating misinformation is difficult because social media networks are large, dynamic, and unpredictable. A platform may need to decide which posts, users, or interactions to review, label, downrank, remove, or restrict, while operating under limited resources and policy constraints. Different interventions may have different costs and effects, and applying an intervention to one part of the network may influence how misinformation spreads elsewhere. Therefore, misinformation mitigation can be viewed as a combinatorial optimisation problem: given a large set of possible interventions, the goal is to select a feasible set that reduces the spread of misinformation as much as possible while respecting relevant constraints.

Constraint programming is a paradigm for modelling and solving combinatorial search and optimisation problems. It represents a problem in terms of variables, domains, and constraints, and then searches for assignments that satisfy the constraints and optimise a given objective~\cite{rossiHandbookConstraintProgramming2006}. Constraint programming has been applied to a wide range of problems, including scheduling~\cite{RahmanClassScheduling2020}, resource allocation~\cite{Cano2018TaskVariantAllocation}, maximisation~\cite{ReginMaximum2003}, and minimisation~\cite{gualandi2011exact}. These characteristics make it suitable for modelling misinformation mitigation, where decisions must be made among many possible actions subject to limits such as intervention budgets, user restrictions, timing constraints, and platform policies.

This thesis proposes a constraint-programming-based algorithm for mitigating the spread of misinformation on social media platforms. The algorithm models possible mitigation actions as decision variables and uses constraints to represent the limitations under which a platform must operate. The objective is to identify intervention strategies that reduce the spread of misinformation while satisfying these constraints.